        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, you can paint over wallpaper if it is firmly stuck to the wall, it is a paper or paper-backed product rather than slick vinyl, and every seam and edge is glued down before you start. The non-negotiable step is sealing it with an oil-based or shellac-based primer first, because the water in latex primer and paint can soften the old paste and make the paper bubble and lift. Get those conditions right and the paint job will hold for years; miss one and you will be scraping blistered paint and soggy paper off the wall within a week. If the paper is loose, layered, vinyl or in a steamy bathroom, stripping it is less work in the long run.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. So Is Painting Over Wallpaper Actually a Good Idea?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It is a legitimate technique, not a hack, and painters have used it for decades on walls where removal would do more harm than good. The paint is not really bonding to the wallpaper at all. It is bonding to the primer, and the primer is bonding to the paper's surface. That makes the whole job only as strong as the weakest layer underneath, which is the old wallpaper paste. If that paste is still holding the paper tight everywhere, the finished wall behaves like any other painted wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case for painting over is strongest when stripping would damage the wall. Plenty of houses built from the 1970s onward had wallpaper hung straight onto bare, unprimed drywall. When you soak and scrape that paper, the paste has bonded to the drywall's own paper face, and the two come off together. You end up with torn brown paper, fuzzy patches and hours of skim coating. In that situation, sealing the wallpaper in place and painting over it is often the cleaner result, not the lazy one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case against is that you are building a finish on top of something you do not control. Wallpaper paste is water-soluble by design, so moisture is its enemy forever. Seams that look flat now can telegraph through paint, especially under sheen and raking light. And if you or a future owner ever do want the paper gone, it is now harder to remove, because the paint and primer block the water and remover solutions that normally soften the paste.
          </p>

          <p>
            So our honest verdict is conditional. On a sound, plain paper in a dry room, painted with a proper sealing primer, the result can be indistinguishable from a normal wall to a casual eye. On loose, vinyl, multi-layer or bathroom paper, painting over it is a shortcut that usually costs more time than it saves. The next section helps you work out which group your wall is in before you open a single can.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Tight paper, glued seams, solvent-based primer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you cannot tick all three, either fix the paper first or strip it. Skipping any one of them is how most painted-wallpaper failures start.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="does-it-qualify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Do You Know If Your Wallpaper Can Be Painted?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with adhesion, because it decides everything else. Walk the room with a flashlight held flat against the wall and look for bubbles, ridges and lifting edges. Press the paper firmly with the flat of your hand across every area, listening for a crackle and feeling for hollow spots. Pay particular attention to the top edge near the ceiling, around outlets and switch plates, behind where furniture sat, and along any exterior wall that gets cold. Those are the places paste fails first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, check the seams one by one. Run a fingernail or the tip of a putty knife gently along each vertical joint. If the seam lifts even slightly, it will lift more once wet paint shrinks across it. A handful of loose seams is normal and fixable with seam adhesive. If seams are lifting all over the room, or long sections pull away as you test them, the paste has failed broadly and painting over it is building on sand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then work out what the paper actually is. Find an inconspicuous corner, lift a seam with a knife, and try to peel the top layer away. If a thin plastic-feeling face peels off and leaves a paper backing on the wall, it is a peelable vinyl or vinyl-coated paper. If it tears as a single sheet of paper, it is a traditional printed paper. If the surface feels slick, wipes clean with a damp cloth and water beads on it, you are almost certainly looking at vinyl.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, count the layers. Look at the edge of a switch plate cutout or a torn corner. Older houses often have two, three or more papers stacked up, each one hung over the last. Every extra layer is another bond line that can fail, and the weight of paint and primer adds stress to all of them. One layer of well-stuck paper is a good candidate. Several layers is a strong signal to strip.
          </p>

