        <div id="why-primer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Can't You Use Regular Water-Based Primer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most wallpaper paste is water-activated. Traditional wheat and cellulose pastes, and the clay and starch adhesives on many prepasted papers, are designed to soften when wet so the paper can be hung and repositioned. When you roll a water-based primer or latex paint directly onto the paper, the paper absorbs water, swells, and that moisture reaches the paste behind it. The paste softens again, the swollen paper loses its grip, and you get bubbles that can appear within minutes of rolling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some of those bubbles shrink back flat as the paper dries, which is why people sometimes report that latex worked fine. Others do not. Seams are especially vulnerable because the paper edges absorb water from two directions and curl. Even when the first coat looks fine, the paper has been stressed, and a second wet coat or a humid week can finish the job. It is an avoidable gamble when the fix is simply choosing a different primer.
          </p>

          <p>
            An oil-based alkyd primer or a pigmented shellac primer puts no water into the paper. Both form a solvent-resistant film that seals the surface, so the latex paint you apply afterward never reaches the paper or the paste. Shellac is the stronger choice when the paper has a bold pattern, ink that might bleed, or water stains, because it is the best stain blocker in common use. Oil-based primer is cheaper per gallon and perfectly good on plain paper.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both come with trade-offs. Shellac primer is thin, smells strong of alcohol, and needs denatured alcohol or ammonia solution for cleanup, but it is often dry enough to recoat in about an hour. Oil-based primer has a heavier solvent smell, needs mineral spirits for cleanup, and usually wants several hours to overnight before topcoating. Ventilate well with either and follow the label. Our <a href="/best-primer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best primer guide</a> covers the specific shellac and alkyd options we rate for sealing problem surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the sealing primer has dried, the rest is ordinary painting. Any good acrylic latex wall paint goes on top, with the usual two coats. A flat or matte finish is the forgiving choice, because sheen highlights every seam and ripple in the paper underneath. If you want to compare finishes and coverage, the picks in our <a href="/best-interior-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best interior paint roundup</a> apply here once the primer is on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strip-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. When Should You Strip Wallpaper Instead of Painting It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Strip it when the paper is failing. If large sections are bubbled, lifting or crackly under your hand, gluing and patching becomes a patchwork that will show through paint. Paint also adds weight and shrinks slightly as it dries, which pulls on the paper. A wall that is half-holding now will usually be worse in a year, and then you are removing paper, primer and paint together, which is harder than removing paper alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strip it in wet rooms. Bathrooms with a shower or tub, laundry rooms and kitchens over the range all see repeated humidity, and the paste behind the paper is water-soluble. A paint film slows moisture but does not stop it. In those rooms painted wallpaper is the classic source of peeling patches above the shower and at the ceiling line. If the room steams up, take the paper down and paint the wall properly, with a moisture-resistant bathroom paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strip it when you want a top-tier finish or are preparing to sell. Seams have a habit of showing through, especially on walls hit by raking light from a window. A buyer's inspector or a sharp-eyed agent may spot painted-over paper, and some buyers treat it as a hidden job. If the room is a showpiece, or you are investing in a high-sheen finish, a properly stripped, repaired and primed wall is worth the extra weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the paper and paint over it when stripping would wreck the wall. The giveaway is a test patch: score a small area, soak it with warm water or a remover, scrape, and look at what comes off. If the paper releases and leaves clean drywall or plaster, stripping is a reasonable job. If the drywall's brown face paper tears away with the wallpaper, the wall was never primed, and every square foot you strip will need skim coating. That is the situation where painting over earns its place.
          </p>

          <p>
            One safety note applies to older homes. In houses built before 1978, there may be lead paint on the wall under the paper, or between layers of paper. Stripping with water and a scraper is relatively low-dust, but sanding anything in that stack is not. If you are unsure, a simple lead test kit from a hardware store is cheap, and the EPA's lead-safe guidance on dust control applies to any sanding you plan to do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vinyl-textured" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What About Vinyl, Textured or Grasscloth Wallpaper?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinyl is the hardest case. Solid vinyl and vinyl-coated papers are designed to be scrubbable and moisture-resistant, which means they also resist paint adhesion. Latex paint applied directly will often peel in sheets with a fingernail. If you must paint vinyl, scuff it thoroughly with fine sandpaper, clean off the dust, and use a bonding primer or shellac primer rated for glossy surfaces, then test adhesion with tape on a small patch before committing to the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Peelable vinyl is the good news version. Many vinyl papers from the last few decades are built in two layers, a vinyl face laminated onto a paper backing. The face can usually be peeled off dry, often in full-height strips, leaving the backing stuck to the wall. That backing is just paper, and once the face is gone you can treat it like any other plain paper: glue loose edges, seal with oil or shellac primer, then paint. It is often the best of both approaches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Textured and embossed papers are a matter of taste. Anaglypta-style embossed papers and similar heavy relief papers are actually meant to be painted, and they take paint well once primed. Ordinary papers with a raised or flocked pattern are different. Paint does not hide texture; it highlights it. If you paint a textured paper you will have a textured painted wall, and if that is not the look you want, stripping is the only route to a smooth surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grasscloth, fabric-backed and natural fibre papers are poor candidates. They are absorbent, uneven, and often hung with strong adhesives, and painting them tends to look like what it is: a painted woven surface with visible seams and texture. Foil and metallic papers are also difficult because the primer has nothing porous to bite into and the seams are sharp. For any of these, if the goal is a plain painted wall, budget for removal and wall repair rather than paint.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Paint Over Wallpaper, Step by Step?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean first. Wallpaper collects grease, smoke film and dust that interfere with primer adhesion, especially in kitchens and hallways. Wipe the walls with a lightly damp sponge and a mild cleaner, keeping water to a minimum so you are not soaking the paper you are about to save. Let the surface dry completely. Remove switch plates and outlet covers, and protect floors and trim with drop cloths and <a href="/best-painters-tape/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">painter's tape</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fix every loose seam and edge. Use a wallpaper seam adhesive, worked under the lifted edge with a small brush or applicator, then press the seam flat with a seam roller and wipe off any squeeze-out. Glue down the top edge along the ceiling and the bottom edge at the baseboard, as these are where peeling usually starts. Give the adhesive the time its label asks for, usually a day, before priming. If a section is beyond saving, cut it out with a sharp knife and treat it as a patch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prime with an oil-based or shellac primer. Cut in at the edges with a brush and roll the field with a medium nap roller, keeping coats even and avoiding heavy overlaps. One coat is usually enough to seal plain paper; a bold pattern may need a second coat to stop it ghosting through. Ventilate the room well, follow the label recoat time, and do not rush to the next step while the primer still smells strongly or feels tacky.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now deal with seams and damage. If seams are visible after priming, skim them with a thin coat of lightweight joint compound or spackle, feathered out several inches on either side, and sand smooth once dry. Doing this after the sealing primer matters, because joint compound is water-based and you want the primer between it and the paper. Our guide to <a href="/joint-compound-vs-spackle/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">joint compound vs spackle</a> explains which suits wide feathering. Spot-prime the patched areas with the same sealing primer so they do not flash through the paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish with two coats of acrylic latex wall paint. A flat or matte sheen hides the most, eggshell is acceptable on a well-prepared wall, and satin or semi-gloss will show every imperfection. Use a quality roller and a decent brush for cutting in, and keep a wet edge to avoid lap marks. Wait the full recoat time between coats. Then leave the room alone for a few days before hanging heavy art or pushing furniture against the walls.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="long-term" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Happens Down the Road With Painted Wallpaper?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Done well, painted wallpaper is durable in a normal room. The primer seals the paper from the paint's moisture and from everyday humidity, the paint gives it a washable surface, and there is no particular reason for it to fail as long as the paste behind it stays dry. Many people live with painted-over paper for years without a problem, and you will only know it is there if you look closely at the seams in low light.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weak points do not go away, they just get covered. Seams can open slightly as the house moves through seasons, and corners can crack where two walls meet. A water leak behind the wall, a plumbing drip or a long humid spell will reactivate the paste in that area, and the paper will bubble under the paint. Small bubbles can sometimes be slit with a knife, glued with a syringe of seam adhesive and touched up, but a large area means stripping that section.
          </p>

          <p>
            Removal later is harder, not impossible. The paint and solvent-based primer stop water and wallpaper removers from soaking through, so the usual spray-and-scrape approach does little. The routine is to perforate the surface thoroughly with a scoring tool or coarse sandpaper, then use a wallpaper steamer or a remover solution and give it time to penetrate. Expect it to take noticeably longer than stripping unpainted paper, and expect more wall repair afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the real trade. Painting over sound wallpaper saves you a messy weekend now and may save the drywall entirely, at the cost of a slightly harder job for whoever strips it later. Stripping takes longer up front but leaves a clean slate. If you plan to stay a few years and the paper passes the tests above, painting is a sensible, durable choice. If the paper is marginal, take the time now. For other surface questions in this run of guides, see our explainers on <a href="/can-you-caulk-over-caulk/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether you can caulk over caulk</a> and <a href="/how-long-does-wood-filler-take-to-dry/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long wood filler takes to dry</a> before patching trim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling latex primer straight onto the paper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based primer and paint can soften the paste behind the paper and cause bubbling and lifting seams within minutes. Some bubbles flatten as they dry, others never do. Seal the paper with an oil-based or shellac primer first, then use latex paint on top. That one choice prevents most painted-wallpaper failures we see.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over loose seams and edges</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint shrinks slightly as it cures and pulls on anything not stuck down. A seam that lifts a little before painting will lift more after, and it will now crack the paint film too. Glue every loose seam and the top and bottom edges with seam adhesive, roll them flat, and let them set first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skimming seams before priming</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Joint compound and spackle are water-based, so troweling them straight onto bare paper puts water exactly where you do not want it, at the seams. Prime with a sealing primer first, then skim any visible seams, sand, and spot-prime the patches. The order is what keeps the paper dry and stuck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a high-sheen paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Satin and semi-gloss reflect light and exaggerate every seam, ripple and patch in the paper below. On painted wallpaper, flat or matte is the forgiving choice, and eggshell is the shiniest we would risk on a very well-prepared wall. Save the higher sheens for trim and doors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting wallpaper in a shower bathroom</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paste is water-soluble and a paint film is not a perfect moisture barrier. Daily steam eventually reaches the paste, and painted paper starts peeling above the shower and along the ceiling line. In wet rooms, strip the paper, repair the wall, and use a proper moisture-resistant paint.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of primer should I use over wallpaper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use an oil-based alkyd primer or a pigmented shellac primer. Both seal the paper without putting water into it, so the paste behind stays put. Shellac is the better pick over bold patterns, inks that could bleed or water stains, because it blocks stains most reliably. Oil-based primer is cheaper and fine on plain paper. Avoid water-based primer as the first coat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over wallpaper without primer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but it is a gamble we would not take. Latex paint applied directly can soak the paper and soften the paste, causing bubbles and lifting seams, and patterns or inks may bleed through. Even when it looks fine at first, adhesion is weaker. A single coat of sealing primer costs little compared with redoing a failed wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will wallpaper seams show through paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They often do, especially under sheen and in light that rakes across the wall from a window. To minimise them, glue seams flat, prime, then skim each seam with a thin layer of joint compound feathered several inches wide, sand smooth and spot-prime. A flat or matte paint finish hides what remains far better than eggshell or satin.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I fix bubbles that appear after painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If bubbles appear while the paint is wet, wait for it to dry; some shrink back flat. For bubbles that remain, slit each one with a sharp knife, inject seam adhesive with a glue syringe, press flat and wipe clean. Once dry, skim lightly if needed, spot-prime with shellac or oil primer, and touch up with paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to paint over wallpaper or remove it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Painting over is cheaper in time and materials if the paper is sound, since you skip removal tools and wall repair. Removal costs more up front but avoids future problems. The exception is paper on unprimed drywall, where stripping tears the wall surface and repair costs climb, making painting over the cheaper and often better option.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over peel-and-stick wallpaper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We would not. Peel-and-stick products are usually vinyl with a pressure-sensitive adhesive, designed to come off cleanly. Paint adds weight and tension that can make edges curl, and the slick surface resists adhesion. Because they are made to be removable, peeling them off and painting the wall directly is almost always easier and longer lasting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should primer dry before painting over wallpaper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the can. Shellac primers are often recoatable within about an hour in normal room conditions, while oil-based primers usually need several hours and sometimes overnight. Cold rooms and high humidity stretch those times. The primer should be dry, hard and odour-faint before you topcoat. Rushing latex over soft oil primer can cause wrinkling or poor adhesion.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

