        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Painting over wallpaper works when three things are true: the paper is tight to the wall, it is not slick vinyl, and you seal it with an oil-based or shellac primer before any water-based product touches it. Glue the seams, prime, skim what shows, and finish with a low-sheen paint, and the wall will look and behave like any other painted wall.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If the paper is lifting, layered, vinyl or in a steamy bathroom, stop and strip it instead. The extra weekend now is far less work than chasing bubbles and peeling paint later. And if a test patch shows the drywall tearing as the paper comes off, that is your answer: seal it, paint it and move on.
          </p>
        </div>

